---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'install', label: '1. Install the package' },
   { id: 'check', label: '2. Check your machine' },
   { id: 'run', label: '3. Run axe' },
   { id: 'coverage', label: '4. Check coverage' },
   { id: 'session', label: '5. Start a session' },
   { id: 'read', label: '6. Read the transcript' },
   { id: 'stop', label: '7. Stop the session' },
   { id: 'report', label: 'Claims this run supports' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Quickstart"
   summary="Install the package, confirm your machine is ready, then take one local route from an axe run to a screen reader transcript. It runs on the simulated screen reader, so nothing beyond Node and a running development server is required."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="panel" id="install">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Step 1</p>
         <h2>Install the package</h2>
      </div>
      <pre><code>npm install --global a11ied</code></pre>
      <p>
         The package installs two identical binaries, <code>a1</code> and <code>a11ied</code>.
         Every example on this page uses <code>a1</code>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="check">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Step 2</p>
         <h2>Check your machine</h2>
      </div>
      <pre><code>a1 doctor</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>✓ Ready for a1 commands on macOS 26.5.2 (arm64)

Targets
  ✓ macos-voiceover  ready  Ready for real screen reader sessions.
      ✓ AppleScript control enabled in VoiceOver settings
      ✓ AppleScript control confirmed by macOS
      ✓ VoiceOver local preferences exist
      ✓ VoiceOver welcome dialog suppressed
      ✓ Guidepup VoiceOver preferences bundle installed
      ✓ Screen recording available to the current host app
  - windows-nvda     Only available on Windows.
  ✓ virtual-dom      ready  No setup needed.

Browser automation
  ✓ Google Chrome  ready  Used for a1 axe scans.
      Path    /Applications/Google Chrome.app/Contents/MacOS/Google Chrome
      Launch  system install, opened through the "chrome" browser channel
      Others  Playwright Chromium

  Policy    system-browser-first: an installed Chrome, Edge, Brave, or Chromium is used before Playwright's Chromium
  Fallback  npx playwright install chromium

Environment
  a11ied  0.1.0
  Node    v24.19.0
  npm     11.17.0
  Host    macOS 26.5.2 (arm64) [darwin]</code></pre>
      <p>
         The first line is the verdict. <code>✓</code> means every check passed and you can move
         on. A <code>✗</code> switches to an <strong>Action items</strong> list with the exact
         command that fixes each step. The virtual target this quickstart uses needs no setup, so
         the rest of the page works even when VoiceOver or NVDA still needs one.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="run">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Step 3</p>
            <h2>Run axe on the page</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            Start your development server, then run axe against the route you are working on.
            axe runs the page in a browser it finds automatically, an installed Chrome, Edge,
            Brave, or Chromium, or the Chromium <code>npx playwright install chromium</code>
            downloads.
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 axe http://localhost:3000/checkout --level AA --json</code></pre>
         <p>
            The result has four arrays: <code>violations</code>, <code>passes</code>,
            <code>incomplete</code>, and <code>inapplicable</code>. Each violation lists the axe
            rule, its impact, the failing selector, and the failing HTML. See
            <a href="/guides/violations">understand a violation</a> for what to do with one.
         </p>
         <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"violations": [
  &#123;
    "id": "button-name",
    "impact": "critical",
    "help": "Buttons must have discernible text",
    "nodes": [
      &#123;
        "target": ["button[type=\"button\"]"],
        "html": "&lt;button type=\"button\"&gt;&lt;img src=\"cart.png\" alt=\"\"&gt;&lt;/button&gt;"
      &#125;
    ]
  &#125;
]</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">Empty results</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            If a single-page app has not mounted yet, the result has a <code>warnings</code>
            entry saying the page had too little content to test. Load the route in a browser first
            to confirm it renders.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="coverage">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Step 4</p>
         <h2>Check what the run left out</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         A clean axe run covers the success criteria that have axe rules. Check coverage, whether
         axe can decide a criterion on its own, before you draw a conclusion.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 wcag 2.4.3 --json
a1 wcag 1.4.3 --json</code></pre>
      <p>
         Focus Order returns <code>"coverageState": "hybrid"</code> with an empty
         <code>axeRuleIds</code> list. Contrast (Minimum) returns
         <code>"coverageState": "automated"</code> with <code>color-contrast</code>. The first needs
         a screen reader session. The second is already covered by step 3.
      </p>
      <p>
         <a href="/test-methods">Test methods</a> explains the four states and where the mapping
         data comes from.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="session">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Step 5</p>
            <h2>Start a screen reader session</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            The virtual target loads the URL into a simulated reader. Pass
            <code>--allow-virtual</code> to say the simulation is what you want.
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 sr start --sr virtual --allow-virtual http://localhost:3000/checkout --json</code></pre>
         <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"session": &#123;
  "sessionId": "drv_c33f1d8f4461",
  "target": "virtual",
  "targetType": "simulated",
  "url": "http://localhost:3000/checkout"
&#125;</code></pre>
         <p>
            Every later <code>sr</code> command uses this session. No command takes a session id.
         </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">One session</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            One session is active per user. A second <code>a1 sr start</code> stops the first one
            and reports it in the warnings.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="read">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Step 6</p>
         <h2>Step through the page and read the log</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         <code>sr next</code> moves one item and returns what the reader announced.
         <code>sr read-all</code> steps from the cursor to the end of the document and returns
         the whole transcript in one call.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr read-all --json</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"spokenPhraseLog": [
  "document",
  "heading, Checkout, level 1",
  "form",
  "Email",
  "textbox, Email, required",
  "Card number",
  "textbox, Card number",
  "button, Pay now",
  "button",
  "end of form",
  "end of document"
]</code></pre>
      <p>
         Read the log as a sequence. The email field announces its required state. The second to
         last entry announces as <code>button</code> with nothing after it, which matches the
         <code>button-name</code> violation from step 3.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="stop">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Step 7</p>
         <h2>Stop the session</h2>
      </div>
      <pre><code>a1 sr stop --json</code></pre>
      <p>
         Stopping shuts down the driver process, finishes any recording, and deletes the session
         state file. Sessions outlive the shell that started them, so stop each one you start.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="report">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Result</p>
         <h2>The evidence this run supports</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         You have an axe result for the 73 rules mapped to levels A and AA, and a transcript of
         the announcement order for one route on one target.
      </p>
      <p>
         It is not a conformance claim. WCAG groups its requirements into three conformance
         levels, and this run leaves 12 of level AA's 24 criteria with no automated coverage.
         Move to <a href="/guides/screen-reader">test with a screen reader</a> when the real
         behavior of VoiceOver or NVDA is what you need to check.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
